17 pointer and advanced application

In section 11, we talked about how to use pointer which points to variable as the argument to let function change that variable. In section 12, we talked about how to do arithmetic operation on pointer which points to element of array. In this section, we will talk about the dynamic memory allocation and pointer which points to function.

17.1 dynamic memory allocation

In C lan, data structures are fixed-size, even the flexible array. That maybe introduce problem. C lan also provides the capacity to allocate memory during the running time.

Dynamic memory allocation can be applied to any type data, but mainly to string, array and structure. This type (dma) can be linked to form format, tree and other data structure.

17.1.1 memory allocation function

There are three memory allocation functions in <stdlib.h>:
	malloc()	// Allocate memory without initialization
	calloc() 	// Allocate memory and clear memory
	realloc() 	// resize the memory allocated before

malloc() is the common-used because it doesn't clear the memory. Since function can't aknowledge the specific data type before alloctating memory, it will return void * type, a general pointer.

When there can't be found the desired memory, function will return NULL pointer:
	/* Testin return value */
	p = malloc(10000);
	if (p == NULL) {
		/* alocation failed; take approprite action */
	}

	/* A commen syntex */
	if ((p = malloc(10000)) == NULL) {
		/* alocation failed; take approprite action */
	}

NULL macro is defined in many header, like <locale.h>, <stddef.h>, <stdio.h>, <stdlib.h>, <string.h> and <time.h>

17.2 dynamic allocate string

Dynamic memory allocation is useful for the operation of string. string is stored in character array, and it is hard to predict how long the array need.

	void *malloc(size_t size);

malloc() allocate *size* byte memory, and return the pointer points to that memory. size_t type is the unsigned integer, and because of the sizeof(char) is 1, the malloc() can easily allocate memory for string.

	p = malloc(n + 1)

here p is char * type, void * type will auto convert.

	strcpy(p, "abc");

since the memory allocated by malloc() are not initiated so we can call strcpy() to initiate it.

	char *concat(const char *s1, const char *s2) {
		char * result;
		
		result = malloc(strlen(s1) + strlen(s2) + 1);
		if (result == NULL) {
			printf("Error: malloc failed in concat\n");
			exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
		}
		strcpy(result, s1);
		strcat(result, s2);
		return result;
	}

	// A function call method
	p = concat("abc", "def");

17.3 dynamic allocate array

Since string is equivalent to array, it is also benefit to allocate appropriate size for array. malloc() can be used for allocate memory for array, and calloc() will initiate the memory at the same time. realloc() can "expand" or "shrink" the array as needed.

	// If the program need a n-size array and n can be calculated during the program.
	int *a;
	a = malloc(n * sizeof(int));

once a points to the dynamic allocated memory, one can ignore the fact that a is a pointer and view it as the name of array:
	for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
		a[i] = 0;

The prototype of calloc() in <stdlib.h> is:

	void *calloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size);	

calloc() allocates memory for the array which has nmemb element, in which each element has size long byte. After allocating memory, calloc() will initiate by setting all bit zero.

	a = calloc(n, sizeof(int));

The prototype of realloc() in <stdlib.h> is:

	void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t size);

realloc() can adjust the size of array, ptr must points to the memory that be allocated by malloc(), calloc() or realloc(). size is the new size of array.

17.4 release the memory

The memory allocated by malloc() and other memroy allocation functions comes from a memory sink called heap. heap may be exhausted when frequently call these functions, making them return NULL pointer.

To the program, one memory which can't be accessed is called garbage. One program with garbage may exist memory leak. Some languages provide garbage collector for the location and recycle of garbage, but C-lan doesn't. Each C program takes responsibility of each garbage, use free() to release the memory.

The prototype of free() in <stdlib.h> is:

	void free(void *ptr);

It is easy to use free(): just pass the pointer which points to the unnneed memory.

dangling pointer is the phonemenon that free(p) will release the memory which p points to, but p pointer has not been changed.

17.5 linked list

Dynamic allocation is useful for the structure of sheet, tree, graph and other linked data-type. Linked List is made of a sery of struct, each node has a pointer points to the next node
